<p>Mandan experienced three major growth spurts in its first 150 years. The first came in the late 1870s, when the Northern Pacific Railroad expanded its system by constructing a bridge across the Missouri River and extending its tracks west to Montana. The second followed the completion in the early 1920s of the first automobile bridge across the river between St. Louis and Seattle. Mandan's population surged again in the 1950s after the Standard Oil Company announced plans for a petroleum refinery capable of processing 28,000 barrels per day.</p>

<h3>1951</h3>

<p>The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers completed a study proposing a 100-megawatt hydroelectric power plant near its former Rock Haven snagboat port northeast of Mandan. The site closed to river service in the mid-1930s and was reclaimed by the federal government. The automobile bridge completed in 1922 had allowed truck traffic to complement rail freight, making river transportation obsolete on the upper Missouri River.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1950s/385-water-level-profile-missouri-river-dams.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nifty Fifties - Power, Petroleum &amp; Progress page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The proposed dam, projected to resemble the future Big Bend Dam in South Dakota, generated controversy over its land-use effects, the balance between public and private power generation, and water rights linked to the Garrison Dam project, which was soon to begin construction. Its anticipated benefits were not considered sufficient to justify the cost, and the project was not approved.</p>

<p>Source: <em>Big Dam Era</em>, Missouri River Division, U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.</p>

<h3>1952</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1950s/312-1953-aug-heskett-plt-intake-under-construction-irs-ndsu.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nifty Fifties - Power, Petroleum &amp; Progress page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On May 15, Montana-Dakota Utilities (MDU) announced plans to construct the R. M. Heskett Power Station, a lignite-fired steam-generating plant north of Mandan. The company, which had considered the site since 1932, purchased 125 acres on the southern half of the historic {% person "james-bellows" %}James Bellows{% endperson %} homestead from the Zachmeier family. The estimated construction cost was {% inflation 9000000, 1952 %} for 25 megawatts of generating capacity. The plant, named for a former MDU executive, began operating in 1954. By 1964, it had been expanded to a capacity of 60 megawatts.</p>

<p>Newspaper articles mistakenly reported that {% person "elijah-boley" %}Elijah Boley{% endperson %} had previously owned the site. It had been home to the steamboat port Bellows Landing and was renamed Rock Haven after the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers purchased it for use as a river snagboat support facility.</p>

