<h3>1958</h3>

<h3>1959</h3>

<p>The city was approaching its peak as a commercial center, unaware of the effects that the interstate highway then under construction north of Mandan would bring. The city boasted five grain elevators with a combined capacity of more than 760,000 bushels. Occident Elevator operated two facilities, one in the "Syndicate" area and another along Main Street. Other grain-storage and handling businesses included Slope Grain and Feed Company, the Farmers Union Grain Association, and the Mandan Farmers Co-Op Elevator.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1950s/334-1959-mandan-ben-franklin-store-fire.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nifty Fifties - Power, Petroleum &amp; Progress page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On January 30, firefighters battled for six hours in subfreezing weather to extinguish one of the largest fires in Mandan's history. The Hulette Building, home to the city's Ben Franklin five-and-dime store, was a total loss. Damage was estimated at {% inflation 200000, 1959 %}. Fortunately, no one was killed or seriously injured.</p>

